# Line endings for the two one-click launchers.
#
# cmd.exe is the reason this file exists. A .bat checked out with LF-only line
# endings is read one byte-run at a time by a parser that expects CR before LF,
# and the failure mode is not a syntax error -- it is a `goto` that lands on the
# wrong line, or a label the interpreter cannot find at all. So run.bat is
# pinned to CRLF in the working tree regardless of what core.autocrlf is set to
# on the machine doing the checkout.
#
# run.sh gets the mirror-image treatment: a shell script with CRLF endings dies
# on its own shebang ("/usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory"),
# which is exactly what happens when a Windows checkout is copied onto a Linux
# box or shared into a container.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.sh  text eol=lf
